
########## oxygen.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

        5  [P] from the preprint
       11  [V] verified against a named source
        5  [A] assumed range, swept

      The preprint reports no measured quantity of any kind, so every
      number below is either [V] from the literature or [A] and swept.

1. Oxygen-carrying capacity

      fluid                        vol%    vs human
      human arterial blood        20.00       1.00x
      Cancer magister              3.44        5.8x
      Octopus dofleini             3.06        6.5x
      Nautilus pompilius           2.30        8.7x
      Octopus vulgaris             1.37       14.6x
      cephalopod ceiling           4.50        4.4x

      So hemocyanin carries 4.4 to 14.6 times less oxygen per
      unit volume than human blood, taking the most generous published
      ceiling at one end and a measured octopus at the other.

      And it cannot be fixed by concentrating it.  From the paper the
      preprint's own first citation garbles:

        "a low molecular weight monomeric protein dissolved in the hemolymph is not efficient for oxygen transport"
        "If its concentration in the hemolymph is high enough to transport a significant amount of oxygen, it will yield an unbearably high osmotic pressure."

      Haemoglobin evades that by being packed inside erythrocytes, where it
      is osmotically invisible.  Hemocyanin is dissolved in the haemolymph,
      so its concentration is capped by colloid osmotic pressure and
      viscosity.  The capacity gap is structural, not a matter of degree.

2. What a human resting metabolism requires of the circulation

  [V] resting oxygen consumption           3.5 mL O2/kg/min  (1 MET)
      for a 70 kg adult                  245.0 mL O2/min

      [A] cardiac output at rest      4.0 - 6.0 L/min

        cardiac output    a-v difference required
                4.0 L/min                  6.12 vol%
                6.0 L/min                  4.08 vol%

      For reference, human arterial blood is 20 vol% and venous blood about
      15, so the measured arteriovenous difference at rest is about 5 vol% --
      which is what this calculation reproduces.  The arithmetic is
      therefore calibrated against the system it describes.

3. The same requirement, met with hemocyanin

      Extraction cannot be complete: unloading needs a partial-pressure
      gradient, so some oxygen always returns in the venous fluid.

      [A] extraction fraction         25% - 75%

        capacity  extraction   delivered   cardiac output needed   vs human
         4.50          25%     1.12 vol%               21.8 L/min       4.4x
         4.50          75%     3.38 vol%                7.3 L/min       4.4x
         1.37          25%     0.34 vol%               71.5 L/min      14.6x
         1.37          75%     1.03 vol%               23.8 L/min      14.6x

      At the published cephalopod ceiling and a generous 75% extraction
      a resting human needs 7.3 L/min of cardiac output, above the
      normal resting range; at the measured O. vulgaris capacity it is
      24 to 72 L/min, against a maximal human cardiac
      output of roughly 20-25 L/min in a trained athlete.

      So the proposal fails at rest, before any activity is considered.

4. And activity is where it fails by more

      [A] VO2max as a multiple of resting   8 - 20 x

        exertion          VO2  cardiac output with hemocyanin at the ceiling
            rest       245 mL/min                                        7 L/min
         8x rest      1960 mL/min                                       58 L/min
        20x rest      4900 mL/min                                      145 L/min

      The preprint claims thermoregulatory independence and a lifespan of
      centuries for an organism whose oxygen delivery cannot support it
      sitting still.

5. Why the animals that do use hemocyanin are not a counterexample

      Squid reach mass-specific metabolic rates comparable to mammals, which
      is the strongest argument available for the preprint and is worth
      stating.  They do it by moving far more fluid, and the cost is named
      by the people who measured it:

        "The constraint on power output in squids is clearly not oxygen uptake, but oxygen transport in the blood"

      -- i.e. the limit on a squid is precisely the quantity the preprint
      proposes to adopt.  They also run a Bohr effect of -1.2 to -2.3
      against the human -0.35 to -0.5, venous saturations around 14 %, and
      effectively no aerobic scope.  The largest hemocyanin animal, the
      colossal squid, is an ambush predator with a metabolic rate orders
      below an active squid of the same clade.

6. Protein mass per oxygen carried, for the mechanism behind the gap

      [A] haemoglobin: 4 O2 per 64.5 kDa tetramer =  16.1 kDa per O2
      [A] hemocyanin:  1 O2 per binuclear site, 50-100 kDa per O2
      ratio                                3.1 - 6.2 x the protein mass per O2

      That bracket, 3 to 6 times, is the same size as the measured capacity
      gap of 4.4 to 14.6 times, which is what one would expect if the
      difference is largely stoichiometric and partly osmotic.  Both figures
      here are [A] and this section is an explanation of the gap rather than
      evidence for it; the capacity measurements in Sec. 1 are the evidence.

########## genetics.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

        5  [P] from the preprint
       11  [V] verified against a named source
        5  [A] assumed range, swept

1. Is there a gene to switch on?

  [V] hemocyanin family                hemocyanin/hexamerin + tyrosinase binuclear type-3 copper centre (IPR013788)
      genes of that family in Vertebrata (taxon 7742)       0
      same query restricted to Chordata (taxon 7711)        0

  [V] human genes carrying the tyrosinase domain the hemocyanins grew from:
      TYR, TYRP1, DCT
      and the active site of TYRP1 holds zinc, not
      copper -- "Because zinc ions, in contrast to copper ions, are not
      redox-active" -- so it is not even a latent oxygen binder.

      An epigenetic mechanism can change which of an organism's genes are
      expressed.  It cannot express a gene the lineage does not have.  There
      is nothing here to unlock, which makes this a precondition failure
      rather than a question of degree.

2. How the preprint uses the word

  [P] "epigenetic" and its forms           8 occurrences
      methylation                           0
      histone                               0
      chromatin                             0
      measured quantities reported          0

      So the word carries the argument eight times without any of the three
      mechanisms it names appearing once.  And the reference glossed as the
      "baseline framework for understanding the epigenetic adaptation of
      organisms to extreme environmental metal concentrations" --
      Tchounwou et al. 2012 -- contains:
      "epigenet"                            0 occurrences

3. The molecular bridge the preprint names, and its own source

  [P] ceruloplasmin is "the fundamental human protein for copper transport
      in the blood" and is "structurally related to invertebrate
      hemocyanins"

  [V] Hellman & Gitlin, the cited review, abstract:
        "Ceruloplasmin is a serum ferroxidase"
        "Despite the need for copper in ceruloplasmin function, this protein plays no essential role in the transport or metabolism of this metal."

  [V] ceruloplasmin family             multicopper oxidase, cupredoxin fold (IPR045087)
      hemocyanin family                hemocyanin/hexamerin + tyrosinase binuclear type-3 copper centre (IPR013788)
      domain intersection              empty

      Different superfamily, different fold, different class of copper site:
      ceruloplasmin carries type-1 plus a trinuclear T2/T3 cluster, the
      hemocyanins a binuclear type-3 centre.  And ceruloplasmin's
      physiological role runs through IRON efflux, which is the opposite of
      the direction the preprint needs.  This is its only concrete molecular
      link between humans and hemocyanin, and the cited abstract denies it
      in so many words.

4. Copper is redox-active too, from the preprint's own reference

  [P] the proposal rests on copper binding oxygen "without generating a
      cascade of destructive free radicals" and on "absolute resistance to
      oxidative stress (absence of the Fenton reaction)"

  [V] Tchounwou et al. 2012, the preprint's fourth reference:
        "it is this property of copper that also makes it potentially toxic because the transitions between Cu(II) and Cu(I) can result in the generation of superoxide and hydroxyl radicals."

      Hydroxyl radicals are the species the Fenton reaction is named for.
      The argument for replacing iron is that it generates them; the cited
      source attributes the same chemistry to copper. Both metals are redox
      couples, which is why both can carry oxygen and why neither is inert.

5. The one real precedent, which runs the other way

      This is the strongest thing available to the proposal and the preprint
      does not cite it.  Pigment class really has been replaced in an animal
      lineage:

  [V]   "snails of the family Planorbidae are unique among gastropods in that they have replaced the ancestral (blue) hemocyanin as an oxygen-carrier with secondarily evolved hemoglobin"

      So a switch is possible in principle.  Two problems.  The direction is
      from hemocyanin TO haemoglobin, and the reason given is the one this
      whole repository measures:

  [V]   "B. glabrata hemoglobin evolved from pulmonate myoglobin, possibly to replace a less-efficient hemocyanin"

      And it is not reversible:
  [V]   "Once lost, hemocyanin was not regained."

      Three further findings that genuinely support parts of the idea, also
      uncited by the preprint, are recorded in PAPER.md Sec. 6: hemocyanin
      genes persist in the genome after a switch, the same cell type makes
      either pigment in different snails, and hemocyanin expression is
      upregulated up to tenfold under hypoxia.  None of them reaches across
      to a lineage that has no hemocyanin gene at all.
